Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["IDM In Twits", "Chapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8795699234855127`*^9, 
  3.879569962119452*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b18b733d-e323-4e8f-a580-\
11e67cecdc75"],

Cell["\<\
Studied (vs read) N. FUREY\[CloseCurlyQuote]s two 2022 Papers and Theses, \
find here https://www.furey.space/ 

Furey gets \[OpenCurlyQuote]standard model\[CloseCurlyQuote] from a Division \
Algieba: Deeper in go deeper find in own mind space.

The work of Malcolm H. MacGregor is also deep in own mind space. MacGregor \
quantized particle lifetimes and masses with the Alpha Constant. \
https://www.amazon.com/Power-Alpha-Elementary-Generation-quantized/dp/\
9812569618

Something can be learned by connecting Furey and MacGregor work via the \
\[OpenCurlyQuote]particles\[CloseCurlyQuote] from the Division Algieba and \
the quantized lifetimes and masses from MacGregor. To start with the Alpha \
Constant should be hidden in Furey\[CloseCurlyQuote]s work.

Connections can also be to MacGregor\[CloseCurlyQuote]s work in chapter five \
up: The Particle-Hole Pair Mathology. Viewed in Spinor context can map back \
to Furey.

MacGregor notes mass of spinning particle 1.5 times mass of non-spinning \
particle. Assumption: If spinning a Hole would have a Mass of 1/2 +/-, both \
in effect for net of zero, needs validation. 1/2 always interesting.

One pass look found \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]ON EIGHT KINDS OF SPINORS\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]
Andrzej Trautman: \
https://inspirehep.net/files/bf6fb44df637044a770069f057430be8 Only scanned, \
looks like can be mapped to both Furey and MacGregor.

\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8795734135498133`*^9, 3.879573421668116*^9}, {
   3.8795735533033648`*^9, 3.879573606083818*^9}, {3.8795736779881287`*^9, 
